[[Return to the corridor|warrior_queen_day_4_12]]


:: warrior_queen_day_4_12 {"position":"850,19475","size":"100,100"}
The corridor from the Iron Garden to the main castle was cool and dim. Arrow-slit windows threw bars of light across the flagstones. My boots   slightly loose now, the feet having narrowed   echoed with each step. I passed the entrance to the Great Library and paused.

Through the heavy door, half-ajar, I could hear the scratch of a quill. A throat cleared. The rustle of parchment.

Jeral.

I pushed the door open. The library's vast interior stretched before me   shelves climbing three storeys, ladders and catwalks and the dusty, breathing hush of ten thousand books existing in quiet communion. Jeral sat at his customary desk near the window, surrounded by scrolls, his spectacles perched on the very tip of his thin nose in a position that defied both gravity and good sense. He looked up.

<<speech "jeral">>Your Highness. You appear to have been shot.<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>Nearly. By myself, in a manner of speaking.<</speech>>

I leaned against the doorframe. My arms ached. My shoulder burned. The breast throbbed. But there was comfort in Jeral's presence   the man was a pool of calm in a castle of storms. Nothing rattled him. Nothing surprised him. He existed in a state of perpetual, gentle expectation that everything would be exactly as complicated as it always was, and his only task was to record the complications accurately.

<<speech "jeral">>Dame Yseldra, I presume. I noted her arrival this morning. She is not a woman who enters a building quietly, despite all evidence suggesting she believes she does.<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>She taught me the feminine draw. The archery form for... for someone with...<</speech>>

I gestured vaguely at my chest. Jeral's eyes did not flicker. Did not drop. Did not acknowledge the gesture with anything other than an infinitesimal adjustment of his spectacles.

<<speech "jeral">>For someone whose anatomy has changed in ways that affect the string's path. Yes. I have read about the feminine draw in Lady Orenthia's //Treatise on the Queen's Blades//. Third floor, east alcove, second shelf from the top. Published three hundred years ago and largely ignored since, which is the fate of most texts written by women about women's combat techniques. Dame Yseldra is one of perhaps four people in the kingdom who still practice it.<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>You knew she was coming.<</speech>>

<<speech "jeral">>I knew Sir Aldric had requested her presence through the duty roster, which I maintain. I drew the obvious inference. It is not difficult when one pays attention, which most people decline to do on principle.<</speech>>

He set down his quill. Folded his hands. The gesture was precise, symmetrical   Jeral's equivalent of opening a door and inviting you in.

<<speech "jeral">>How did you find it? The new draw?<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>Alien. Wrong. Like trying to shoot with someone else's arms attached to my body.<</speech>>

<<speech "jeral">>Interesting. That is precisely how Lady Orenthia describes the male draw when performed by a woman. Quote: //The form demands shoulders I do not possess and a chest I cannot eliminate.// End quote. She found the feminine draw, by contrast, to be a natural extension of the body she //did// possess. The same muscles, the same bones, but arranged around the anatomy rather than against it.<</speech>>

He paused. Adjusted his spectacles again.

<<speech "jeral">>You may find, Your Highness, that many things which feel alien now will feel natural in time. Not because you have forced yourself to accept them, but because the body you are growing into was designed for them from the start. The discomfort is the gap between where you are and where you are going. It narrows. It does not vanish entirely   I suspect no transition is ever entirely seamless   but it narrows.<</speech>>

He picked up his quill. The door had closed. The conversation was over. But his words stayed with me as I walked the rest of the corridor, carried alongside the ache in my breast and the exhaustion in my arms and the strange, growing sense that the body I was fighting against might, eventually, become the body I was fighting //with//.

[[Find Aldric at afternoon training|warrior_queen_day_4_13]]


:: warrior_queen_day_4_13 {"position":"975,19475","size":"100,100"}
Aldric was waiting in the Iron Garden when I returned after the midday meal. The sun had climbed high enough to banish the mist entirely, and the cobblestones were dry and warm beneath my boots. He stood at the yard's centre with his arms folded   of course   and Frostfang sheathed   also of course. The man was a monument to consistency.

<<speech "aldric">>How was the archery?<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>I was introduced to the concept that bowstrings and breasts are poorly acquainted and prefer to keep it that way.<</speech>>

<<speech "aldric">>Good. The lessons that bruise are the ones that stay. Now   the rapier.<</speech>>

He drew his practice blade. I drew the rapier. We assumed positions   Aldric in the modified stance he used to simulate a standard opponent, I in the feminine guard, weight pooled in the hips, core engaged, breath in the belly.

<<speech "aldric">>Today you learn to fight while tired. Every muscle in your body is protesting from the archery. Your shoulder is weakened. Your chest is sore. Your hands are shaking. This is what combat feels like   not the fresh, eager clash of the training yard, but the grinding, exhausted struggle of a second engagement when the first has already taken everything you had.<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>You are a deeply encouraging teacher, Aldric.<</speech>>

<<speech "aldric">>I am not paid to encourage. I am paid to ensure you do not die. Attack me.<</speech>>

I attacked.

The first lunge was sloppy   the shoulder protested, the arm extending with a tremor that rippled through the wrist and into the blade's point. Aldric parried without effort. I cross-stepped right, but my trailing foot dragged   the legs tired from the archery's low stance-hold   and the cross-step became a stumble that left me open.

Aldric's practice blade tapped my ribs. Gently. A touch, not a strike. But the message was clear: //You are dead. That was your heart.//.

<<speech "aldric">>Again.<</speech>>

We drilled. The exhaustion was a living thing   a weight draped over my shoulders and pooled in my legs, dragging at every movement. The rapier, which had felt light as a whisper yesterday, now felt like holding a torch at arm's length. The feminine guard, which had been becoming natural, now required conscious effort to maintain as the core muscles, already fatigued from two hours of archery stance-work, cramped and trembled.

But the body adapted. It always adapted. Not gracefully   grace was a luxury reserved for well-rested limbs   but //stubbornly//. The cross-step found its rhythm despite the dragging foot. The lunge straightened despite the protesting shoulder. The breath found the belly despite the chest that wanted to heave and the breasts   the breasts, which had been an obstacle all morning, which had caught the bowstring and throbbed and ached and demanded accommodation   became, in the strange economy of exhaustion, //background//. Too tired to fixate on them. Too focused on the blade and the footwork and the survival of Aldric's measured assault to catalogue every shift and ache and pulse of tender tissue. They were there. They were part of the system. They moved within the cups and the body compensated and the rapier kept moving.

For thirty minutes, I was not a prince fighting breasts. I was a warrior fighting fatigue and the difference, I realised as Aldric's practice blade tapped my shoulder for the seventh time and I staggered backward and reset the guard with arms that trembled like leaves in a gale   the difference mattered.

<<speech "aldric">>Enough. You are done for the day.<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>I can continue  <</speech>>

<<speech "aldric">>You cannot. Your form has degraded past the point of useful practice. Continuing will reinforce bad habits, not build good ones. Rest. Tend the welt   I saw the string take you this morning.<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>You saw  <</speech>>

<<speech "aldric">>I see everything that happens in my yard. Including the squeak.<</speech>>

My face ignited. Aldric's expression remained granite. But somewhere behind those grey eyes, in a room he kept locked and guarded, something was laughing.

<<speech "aldric">>Tomorrow. Dawn. The rapier and the bow together. Yseldra and I shall run the combined drill.<</speech>>

He walked away. Frostfang did not rattle. The man's silence preceded him and followed him and filled the spaces between, and I stood in the sun-warmed yard with a rapier in my shaking hand and a breast that throbbed and a face that burned and the grudging, reluctant, infuriating acknowledgment that the squeak had been genuinely //funny//.

[[Return to the chamber|warrior_queen_day_4_14]]


:: warrior_queen_day_4_14 {"position":"1100,19475","size":"100,100"}
The corridor to my chamber felt longer than usual   perhaps because each step sent a small jolt through the chest that reminded the left breast of its quarrel with bowstrings, or perhaps because the exhaustion had turned my legs into columns of wet sand that moved only under extreme protest.

I reached my chamber door and found a small parcel waiting on the threshold. Wrapped in cloth, tied with ribbon, no note.

I picked it up. It was soft. Pliable. Leather.

Inside was a breast guard. Boiled leather, dark brown, shaped to cup and compress. Two molded shells connected by a leather bridge, with straps that would cross over the shoulders and buckle at the back. The interior was lined with lamb's wool   soft, cushioning, designed to cradle the tissue without crushing it.

Yseldra had said the armourer would have it ready by //tomorrow//. It was here //today//. Which meant she had spoken to the armourer //before// the training session. Which meant she had anticipated the bowstring impact, anticipated the welt, anticipated the squeak and the pain and the lesson   and had prepared the remedy in advance.

