Mistress Veletia Roswyn entered the Iron Garden the way a sunrise enters a valley   gradually, inevitably, and with an intensity of colour that made everything else look grey by comparison. She was resplendent. The word existed for women like Veletia   women who wore silk the colour of crushed elderberries at dawn in a training yard and made it look like a deliberate aesthetic choice rather than a catastrophic misjudgment of venue. Her fiery red curls were piled high and pinned with what appeared to be two golden hairpins shaped like miniature swords   a concession, presumably, to the martial setting. She carried a garment over one arm with the reverence a priestess might reserve for a sacred relic.

