Prima guard. Lunge. Recover. Cross-step left. The movements flowed. Not perfectly   there were hitches, hesitations, moments where old muscle memory tried to reassert itself and the feminine guard wobbled like a compass needle seeking north. But beneath the wobble, the current was there. The flow. The body's growing understanding of its own mechanics   the way the hips drove the pivot, the way the core stabilised the torso, the way the breath anchored in the belly and held everything steady while the limbs moved.

Seconda guard. Parry an imaginary overhead. Pivot-retreat. The retreat carried me in a spiral that sent my braid swinging behind me like a counterweight. The hair had mass   more than the prince's cropped cut had ever possessed   and in motion, it contributed its own micro-adjustments to the body's balance. A new variable. Another thing to account for. But the hair also served a purpose: the swing of the braid told me the speed of the rotation, the quality of the pivot, the clean execution of the hip-driven turn. When the braid swung smooth, the pivot was clean. When it jerked or stalled, something in the footwork was wrong.

I was using my hair as a diagnostic instrument. The prince would have been mortified. The person I was becoming found it... practical.

Terza guard. Drop-step. Rise into thrust. The drop-step was becoming //mine//. Not Aldric's technique performed by Caelan's body, but //Caelan's// technique. The folding was sharper. The recovery faster. The thrust at the apex rose with a fluidity that the first five days of training had not possessed   the body learning to trust the architecture, trusting the wider hips to catch the weight, trusting the lower centre to recover from the descent, trusting the corset-engaged core to power the ascent.

I drilled in the moonlight for an hour. Alone. With no eyes upon me and no expectations save my own.

Something loosened in my chest that was not the corset. Something beneath the boning, beneath the breast guard, beneath the tissue and the ribs and the growing flesh   something //emotional//, something that had been held tight since the transformation began. A knot I had not known I was carrying. A fist I had not known I was clenching.

