The Castle Courtyard was larger than the Iron Garden   a broad, open space ringed by the castle's inner walls, overlooked by the ramparts, the kitchen wing, the armoury, and the Bloom Room's high windows. It was a public space. A visible space. A space where acts were witnessed and reputations forged.

An audience had gathered. Not invited   Aldric's glare would have deterred any formal assembly. But word had spread through the castle's network of servants and soldiers and gossip with the speed of fire through dry grass, and by the time I arrived, the courtyard's edges were lined.

Soldiers on the wall. Servants crossing the yard who had found reasons to linger. Two kitchen girls with flour on their aprons. A farrier leading a horse that suddenly required attention in the most convenient possible location. Jeral at the kitchen entrance, a scroll clutched to his thin chest, spectacles catching the sun and   I looked up   Veletia in the Bloom Room window, her fiery curls unmistakable even at this distance, her hands clasped before her in an attitude that was either prayer or theatrical anticipation and in the shadows of the armoury archway, barely visible, a figure I had not expected.

Torin. He stood apart from the crowd, as he always did   leaning against the stone with his arms crossed, his grey eyes fixed on the courtyard with the focused intensity of a hawk watching a field. He wore his guard's leathers, the Velmoria crest at his shoulder, and his face   strong-jawed, sun-darkened, unreadable   was turned toward me with an attention that felt like heat.

I looked away. The courtyard demanded my focus. Sir Cedric Rowenblade waited at the yard's centre. He was everything a knight of the old school should be   silver-haired, straight-backed, his practice sword held in a hand that had wielded it for thirty years. His armour was polished. His expression was warm and grave and dignified.

